Tests VP6 decoding of inter (P) frames, in particular motion compensation:
 - the chroma motion vector of 4MV macroblocks must be rounded, not truncated
 - the loop filter in motion compensation must only run if the frame
   header enables it (which simple profile streams never do)

The video is 1 keyframe followed by 15 inter frames, simple profile, VP6.0,
256x144 at 8 fps, with a moving camera, so most macroblocks have non-zero
motion vectors, and many of them use 4MV.

The clip is 2 seconds (from 0:49) of Big Buck Bunny,
(c) copyright 2008, Blender Foundation / www.bigbuckbunny.org,
licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 license.
Source: https://download.blender.org/peach/bigbuckbunny_movies/BigBuckBunny_640x360.m4v.zip

Encoded with nihav-encoder (https://nihav.org/vp6enc.html):

ffmpeg -ss 49 -i BigBuckBunny_640x360.m4v -an -vf "fps=8,scale=256:144:flags=lanczos,format=yuv420p" -frames:v 16 bbb.y4m
nihav-encoder --input bbb.y4m --output bbb.flv -an --ostream0 encoder=vp6f,key_int=128,quant=40
ffmpeg -i bbb.flv -vcodec copy -r 8 test.swf

Note that key_int=0 ("automatic") would make every frame a keyframe.

The NihAV encoder itself reconstructs reference frames with the same two
quirks as the (unfixed) decoder, so the stream decodes differently in
reference decoders (FFmpeg, Flash Player). This only amplifies the
difference, which is good for this test.
